nd if something is left you can make the changes required.\r\n486: \r\n487: ## AI:\r\n488: \r\n489: Priyanshu, your pushback is valid and I'll address it directly. No subagent needed for the checklist — I'll track every point you raised inline. Let me be blunt.\r\n490: \r\n491: ---\r\n492: \r\n493: ## You're Right to Challenge the ADHD Suggestion\r\n494: \r\n495: Your criticism is fair: **the DSM-5 ADHD criteria are broad enough that half the screen-addicted 22-year-olds on the planet could meet them.** Zones out? Check. Loses things? Check. Procrastinates? Check. That describes most of Gen Z with a phone addiction. I was NOT diagnosing you — I was saying the symptom overlap exists and needs to be separated from lifestyle damage. Those are different statements.\r\n496: \r\n497: **What I can't do:** I'm a language model. I don't have access to your developmental history, I can't administer neuropsychological tests, and I can't observe you in real life. I'm pattern-matching against published criteria and research. That has value for pointing directions, but it cannot replace a clinician sitting with you for 2 hours.\r\n498: \r\n499: **What your pushback revealed that's actually useful:** You're thinking critically about this. That itself is information — people in cognitive decline don't usually analyze the quality of their diagnostic process.\r\n500: \r\n501: ---\r\n502: \r\n503: ## The \"Blow My Mind\" ADHD Symptoms You Didn't Mention\r\n504: \r\n505: You asked: if you have ADHD, what symptoms would I predict that you haven't told me? Here's the honest test. **Check which ones hit:**\r\n506: \r\n507: | # | Symptom You Didn't Mention | What It Feels Like |\r\n508: |---|---|---|\r\n509: | 1 | **Time blindness** | You sit down for \"10 minutes\" and 2 hours vanish. You genuinely can't estimate how long things take. You're chronically late not because you don't care, but because time moves differently in your head. |\r\n510: | 2 | **Hyperfocus paradox** | You CAN'T focus on boring things for 5 minutes — but you CAN lock into something interesting for 6 hours straight without eating, drinking, or peeing. People say \"you can focus when you want to\" and it makes you angry because that's not how it works. |\r\n511: | 3 | **Emotional flash-reactions** | Intense frustration or anger that spikes hard and fades fast. Someone says something mildly annoying and you react at a 7/10 internally, then 3 minutes later you're fine and confused about why you reacted that way. |\r\n512: | 4 | **Rejection sensitivity** | Criticism — even mild, constructive feedback — hits like a punch. You replay social interactions in your head worrying about whether people think you're stupid. A read-but-not-replied message can spiral your mood. |\r\n513: | 5 | **Starting many, finishing few** | A graveyard of abandoned hobbies, side projects, apps half-learned, courses 30% completed. You get excited about something new, go hard for 1-3 weeks, then lose interest completely. |\r\n514: | 6 | **The \"night owl\" brain** | Your brain turns ON at night. 10 PM–2 AM is when you feel most alive and creative. Morning feels like swimming through mud. (This one you already confirmed — ADHD is strongly associated with delayed circadian rhythm.) |\r\n515: | 7 | **Stimulant paradox** | Coffee or energy drinks calm you down instead of amping you up. Or they have no effect at all. (In ADHD brains, stimulants increase dopamine to normal levels rather than above normal.) |\r\n516: | 8 | **Mental jukebox** | A song, phrase, or random thought loop plays in your head on repeat for hours. You can't turn it off. |\r\n517: | 9 | **The \"wall of awful\"** | You WANT to do a task. You KNOW how to do it. It would take 15 minutes. But starting feels like pushing through an invisible wall. Not laziness — a physical inability to initiate. Then you do it in 10 minutes and wonder why it was so hard to start. |\r\n518: | 10 | **Transition difficulty** | Switching from one activity to another is